+1.2 For $z=x+iy$ and $w=u+iv$, expansion gives $$ |zw|^2=(xu-yv)^2+(xv+yu)^2=(x^2+y^2)(u^2+v^2)=|z|^2|w|^2. $$ Both moduli are nonnegative by [L2], so uniqueness of nonnegative square roots [L3] gives $|zw|=|z||w|$. Also, if $0\le a\le b$ and $r=\sqrt a$, $s=\sqrt b$, then $r,s\ge0$. If $r>s$, then $a-b=r^2-s^2=(r-s)(r+s)>0$, a contradiction. Thus the square-root map is increasing on nonnegative inputs. [given, L2, L3, algebra]
 
