+2.1 The identity $$ (x^2+y^2)(u^2+v^2)-(xu+yv)^2=(xv-yu)^2\ge0. $$ Thus $(xu+yv)^2\le|z|^2|w|^2$. The increasing property proved in step 1.2 gives $|xu+yv|\le|z||w|$, hence $xu+yv\le|z||w|$. Thus $|z+w|^2=|z|^2+|w|^2+2(xu+yv)\le(|z|+|w|)^2$; applying the same increasing property gives $|z+w|\le|z|+|w|$. By the reciprocal formula in [L2], $w(1/w)=(u+iv)(u-iv)/(u^2+v^2)=1$. Step 1.2 gives $|w||1/w|=|1|=1$. Here $|w|>0$: otherwise $x^2+y^2=0$ forces $x=y=0$ because each square is nonnegative, contrary to $w\ne0$. Hence $|1/w|=1/|w|$, and another application of step 1.2 yields $|z/w|=|z|/|w|$. [step 1.2, L2, L3, algebra] ∎
